  "question": {
    "text": "Let $y=y(x)$ be the solution of the differential equation $\\frac{d y}{d x}+3\\left(\\tan ^2 x\\right) y+3 y=\\sec ^2 x$<br/>$y(0)=\\frac{1}{3}+e^3$. Then $y\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ is equal to",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "Sz09K",
      "text": "$\\frac{2}{3}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "wa5Ip",
      "text": "$\\frac{4}{3}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "l7uXK",
      "text": "$\\frac{4}{3}+\\mathrm{e}^3$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "cOKoD",
      "text": "$\\frac{2}{3}+\\mathrm{e}^3$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\begin{aligned}<br/>& \\frac{d y}{d x}+3\\left(\\sec ^2 x\\right) y=\\sec ^2 x, y(0)=\\frac{1}{3}+e^3 \\\\\n& \\text { If }=e^{3 \\int \\sec ^2 x d x}=e^{3 \\tan x}<br/>\\end{aligned}$<br/>$\\therefore$ Solution is<br/>$\\begin{aligned}<br/>& e^{3 \\tan x} y=\\int e^{3 \\tan x} \\sec ^2 x d x \\\\\n& e^{3 \\tan x} y=\\frac{e^{3 \\tan x}}{3}+c \\\\\n& \\because y(0)=\\frac{1}{3}+e^3 \\Rightarrow c=e^3 \\\\\n& \\therefore y\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\frac{e^3}{3}+e^3}{e^3}=\\frac{4}{3}<br/>\\end{aligned}$",
    "image": null
  },
